WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.978
Real work draws on more than one kind of energy.

2
00:00:02.978 --> 00:00:07.255
Physical energy supports movement, presence, and basic stamina.

3
00:00:07.255 --> 00:00:12.907
Cognitive energy supports analysis, memory, planning, and complex decision-making.

4
00:00:12.907 --> 00:00:20.467
Emotional energy supports patience, restraint, empathy, and the ability to handle friction without escalating it.

5
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:27.188
Social energy supports collaboration, communication, facilitation, and relationship maintenance.

6
00:00:27.188 --> 00:00:34.138
Creative energy is the capacity to make something new or solve a problem from an angle that is not obvious yet.

7
00:00:34.138 --> 00:00:41.240
Decision energy supports choices when the answer is uncertain, tradeoffs are real, and consequences matter.

8
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.982
These capacity pools overlap, but they are not identical.

9
00:00:44.982 --> 00:00:50.099
A person may be physically rested and still depleted from difficult decisions.

10
00:00:50.099 --> 00:00:55.674
Another person may have social energy but not enough cognitive room for deep analysis.

11
00:00:55.674 --> 00:00:58.805
Different work types create different demands.

12
00:00:58.805 --> 00:01:04.991
Meetings draw on social and decision energy, especially if they involve conflict or ambiguity.

13
00:01:04.991 --> 00:01:09.497
Deep work draws heavily on cognitive energy and sustained attention.

14
00:01:09.497 --> 00:01:13.850
Troubleshooting combines focus, memory, patience, and judgment.

15
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:18.891
Learning requires attention and enough humility to tolerate not knowing yet.

16
00:01:18.891 --> 00:01:26.069
Administrative work may seem small, but constant small tasks can still drain capacity through fragmentation.

17
00:01:26.069 --> 00:01:29.353
Fatigue does not always announce itself directly.

18
00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:41.420
It can show up as procrastination, irritability, shallow work, careless judgment, repeated checking, avoidance of hard conversations, or a tendency to choose the easiest visible task.

19
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:51.119
Before assuming a motivation problem, it is worth asking what kind of energy the work requires and whether that kind of capacity is actually available.
